e property"] ["text" "the reflexive property"] ["text" "the sum property"] ["text" "the transitive property"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-334685" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the absorption property"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-334685" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-334685" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\text{L2}"] ["math" "\\text{L3}"] ["math" "\\text{L4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-334685" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\text{L4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-334685" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-334685" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "the absorption property"] ["text" "the constant multiple property"] ["text" "the reflexive property"] ["text" "the sum property"] ["text" "the transitive property"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-334685" "field" "field-5" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the constant multiple property"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-334862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $f(x)=4{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$\n- $f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-334862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the following limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=4{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x).\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(4{x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)=x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(4x+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNear $x=0,$ we have $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)≈x,$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x}≈1.\n$$\n\nThus,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(4x+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{x})=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(4+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)≈x$ near $x=0,$ the quotient \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}}≈\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nbecomes arbitrarily large in magnitude as $x\\to 0.$\nHence,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(4+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+x)}{{x}^{2}})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nThus, $f\\ne O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-334862" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4ab6e7e66080a223e0414dfc6b09e9154a7f3f2ed7abf7a43da4d21e201d981"} {"question" "q-334862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-334862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-334862" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-334870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $f(x)=-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-334870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{8}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}}{{x}^{8}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{5}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is false. Let $g(x)={x}^{5}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-5{x}^{6}+9{x}^{4}}{{x}^{5}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-5x+\\frac{9}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O({x}^{5})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-334870" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4ab6e7e66080a223e0414dfc6b09e9154a7f3f2ed7abf7a43da4d21e201d981"} {"question" "q-334870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-334870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-334870" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-335019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f=O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty $\n- $f=O({x}^{3})$ as $x\\to \\infty $" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-335019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose we wish to test whether the following statement is true:\n\n$$\nf(x)=O(g(x))\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nTo do this, we can examine the limit\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to a}\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nWe have the following:\n\n- If the limit is *finite*, the statement is true.\n\n- If the limit is *infinite*, the statement is false.\n\n- If the limit does not exist, the test is inconclusive.\n\nHere, we must consider the polynomial\n\n$$\nf(x)=3{x}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nNow, let us analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Let $g(x)=1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(3{x}^{2}-x) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }x(3x-1) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f\\ne O(1)$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement II is true. Let $g(x)=x{e}^{x}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}-x}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{x(3x-1)}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x-1}{{e}^{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince now direct evaluation leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ we can evaluate the limit using L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3x-1}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3x-1)^{′}}{({e}^{x})^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O(x{e}^{x})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. Let $g(x)={x}^{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{3}{x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f=O({x}^{8})$ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-335019" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "d4ab6e7e66080a223e0414dfc6b09e9154a7f3f2ed7abf7a43da4d21e201d981"} {"question" "q-335019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-335019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-335019" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-335372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following statement. \n\nIf $f(x)={x}^{2}+3{x}^{3},$ then $f=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0.$\n\nA proof of the statement is given below.\n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,f=O({x}^{2}+3{x}^{3})$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,3{x}^{3}=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,f=O({x}^{2})$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,{x}^{2}=O(x)$ as $x\\to 0$ \n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,f=O(x)$  as $x\\to 0$\n\nSelect the correct options to justify each step of the reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L1}$ follows from {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from lines \n{{field-2}} and \n{{field-3}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L5}$ follows from lines {{field-4}}  and \n{{field-5}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-335372" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Suppose $f(x),g(x),h(x)$ and $k(x)$ are functions and $c$ is a nonzero constant. Then, we have the following properties of Big-O notation. \n\n- The sum and product properties. If\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(k)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf+g=O(h+k)\\,\\text{and}\\,fg=O(hk)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The constant multiple property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(cg)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The reflexive property.\n\n$$\nf=O(f)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The transitive property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\n- The absorption property. If\n\n$$\nf=O(g+h)\\,\\text{and}\\,g=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a,\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\nf=O(h)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to a.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let us justify each of the selected steps.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L1},$ which states that\n\n$$\nf=O({{{x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}}_{⏟}}_{f}).\n$$\n\nThis is true by $\\text{the reflexive property}.$\n\n- Next, we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we have\n\n$$\nf=O({x}^{2}+3{x}^{3}),\n$$\n\nand from line $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n3{x}^{3}=O({x}^{2})\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L1 and L2}$ and the $\\text{absorption property},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nf=O({x}^{2})\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Finally, we consider line $\\text{L5}.$ From line  $\\text{L3},$   we have\n\n$$\nf=O({x}^{2})\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0,\n$$\n\nand from line  $\\text{L4},$   we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=O(x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThus, by lines $\\text{L3 and L4}$ and by $\\text{the transitive property},$  we conclude that\n\n$$\nf=O(x)\\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042807/state.json" "file_sha256" "95c4a64cc4117b642f0df6b03498291177a8c9fc691977db2a58f4bfd374575f"} {"question" "q-